### What is the subtree of another tree problem?

Given two binary trees, the main tree and a candidate subtree, determine whether the candidate appears exactly as a subtree somewhere inside the main tree. The match must be exact: same structure and same values at every position.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class ApprovalNode {
    public String role;
    public ApprovalNode left;
    public ApprovalNode right;

    public ApprovalNode(String role) {
        this.role = role;
    }
}
```

### How does the naive approach work?

The outer method visits each node of the main tree. At each node, it runs a same-tree comparison against the candidate. If any comparison succeeds, return true. Otherwise recurse into both children.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.ApprovalNode;

public class SubtreeChecker {
    public static boolean isSubtree(ApprovalNode root, ApprovalNode subRoot) {
        if (root == null) return false;
        if (isSameTree(root, subRoot)) return true;
        return isSubtree(root.left, subRoot) || isSubtree(root.right, subRoot);
    }

    private static boolean isSameTree(ApprovalNode a, ApprovalNode b) {
        if (a == null && b == null) return true;
        if (a == null || b == null) return false;
        return a.role.equals(b.role)
            && isSameTree(a.left, b.left)
            && isSameTree(a.right, b.right);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //     CEO
        //    /   \
        //  VP1   VP2
        //  / \     \
        // D1  D2   D3
        ApprovalNode ceo = new ApprovalNode("CEO");
        ApprovalNode vp1 = new ApprovalNode("VP1");
        ApprovalNode vp2 = new ApprovalNode("VP2");
        ApprovalNode d1 = new ApprovalNode("D1");
        ApprovalNode d2 = new ApprovalNode("D2");
        ApprovalNode d3 = new ApprovalNode("D3");
        ceo.left = vp1; ceo.right = vp2;
        vp1.left = d1; vp1.right = d2;
        vp2.right = d3;

        // Pattern to find
        ApprovalNode pattern = new ApprovalNode("VP1");
        pattern.left = new ApprovalNode("D1");
        pattern.right = new ApprovalNode("D2");

        System.out.println("Is subtree: " + isSubtree(ceo, pattern));
    }
}
```

```text
Is subtree: true
```

### How does the same-tree inner check work?

The inner check is the standard identical-trees comparison. Both null means match. Exactly one null means mismatch. Values must be equal. Both left and right subtrees must recursively match.

```java
private static boolean isSameTree(ApprovalNode a, ApprovalNode b) {
    if (a == null && b == null) return true;   // both ended
    if (a == null || b == null) return false;   // one ended early
    return a.role.equals(b.role)                // values match
        && isSameTree(a.left, b.left)           // left subtrees match
        && isSameTree(a.right, b.right);        // right subtrees match
}
```

### What is the time complexity of the naive approach?

In the worst case, every node of the main tree triggers a full comparison that scans the entire candidate. For a main tree of size n and candidate of size m, this is O(n * m). The space is O(h1 + h2) from the combined recursion depth.

| Approach | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Naive (compare at each node) | O(n * m) | O(h1 + h2) |
| Serialization + KMP | O(n + m) | O(n + m) |

### What is the efficient serialization approach?

Serialize both trees into strings with explicit null markers and delimiters. Then check whether the candidate's serialization is a substring of the main tree's serialization using a linear-time matcher like Knuth-Morris-Pratt.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.ApprovalNode;

public class SubtreeSerialize {
    public static boolean isSubtree(ApprovalNode root, ApprovalNode sub) {
        String mainStr = serialize(root);
        String subStr = serialize(sub);
        return mainStr.contains(subStr); // or use KMP for O(n+m)
    }

    private static String serialize(ApprovalNode node) {
        if (node == null) return ",#"; // null marker with delimiter
        return "," + node.role + serialize(node.left) + serialize(node.right);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApprovalNode main = new ApprovalNode("CEO");
        main.left = new ApprovalNode("VP1");
        main.left.left = new ApprovalNode("D1");
        main.left.right = new ApprovalNode("D2");
        main.right = new ApprovalNode("VP2");

        ApprovalNode sub = new ApprovalNode("VP1");
        sub.left = new ApprovalNode("D1");
        sub.right = new ApprovalNode("D2");

        System.out.println("Serialization match: " + isSubtree(main, sub));
    }
}
```

```text
Serialization match: true
```

### Why are null markers and delimiters essential?

Without explicit null markers, two different tree structures can produce the same serialization. Without delimiters, a value like `12` could be confused with adjacent values `1` and `2`.

```java
// Without null markers: different trees, same string
// Tree A:    1       Tree B:    1
//           /                    \
//          2                      2
// Both serialize to "1,2" without null markers!

// With null markers:
// Tree A: ",1,2,#,#,#"  Tree B: ",1,#,2,#,#"
// Now they are different.

// Without delimiters: value "12" vs "1" followed by "2"
// With comma delimiters: ",12," vs ",1,,2," are distinguishable
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty candidate (always true if we consider null a subtree of any tree), candidate larger than main tree (always false), and identical trees.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.ApprovalNode;

public class SubtreeEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApprovalNode main = new ApprovalNode("CEO");
        main.left = new ApprovalNode("VP");

        // Identical trees
        ApprovalNode same = new ApprovalNode("CEO");
        same.left = new ApprovalNode("VP");
        System.out.println("Identical: " + SubtreeChecker.isSubtree(main, same));

        // Single node match
        System.out.println("Single leaf: " + SubtreeChecker.isSubtree(main, new ApprovalNode("VP")));
    }
}
```

```text
Identical: true
Single leaf: true
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Recognizing both the simple O(n * m) approach and the O(n + m) serialization approach, and knowing the pitfalls of serialization (delimiters, null sentinels, prefix confusion), is the senior-level answer. The serialization approach is faster but more error-prone; the naive approach is safer and often sufficient for small trees.

In production at National Bank of India, compliance maintains templates of known fraudulent approval patterns. The fraud-pattern detector searches for each template inside every branch's full approval hierarchy. When branches are large with many patterns to scan, the serialization approach provides linear performance.

```java
// Fraud pattern detection in production
for (ApprovalNode pattern : knownFraudPatterns) {
    if (SubtreeChecker.isSubtree(branchOrgTree, pattern)) {
        alertService.flagFraudPattern(branchId, pattern);
    }
}
```
